WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:08.960
[Narrator:] Challenge for habilitation: the child with the congenital rubella syndrome.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.400
[Narrator:] A maternal viral infection during pregnancy,

00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:20.434
especially in the first three months,

00:00:20.435 --> 00:00:24.001
has the potential for producing a number of harmful effects

00:00:24.001 --> 00:00:25.767
in the developing fetus.

00:00:25.768 --> 00:00:28.768
An estimated 20,000 to 30,000 children

00:00:28.768 --> 00:00:31.634
were born with the congenital rubella syndrome

00:00:31.635 --> 00:00:35.468
as the result of the 1964-65 rubella epidemic

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.034
in the United States.

00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:40.201
This group of one-and-a-half to four-year-old children

00:00:40.201 --> 00:00:44.001
in the clinic reception area are all in the rubella program

00:00:44.001 --> 00:00:46.934
at the Texas Children's Hospital in Houston.

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:51.335
When the 146 patients were reviewed in 1967,

00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:55.768
90 had hearing loss, 39 were visually impaired,

00:00:55.768 --> 00:00:59.068
and 50 had significant motor handicap.

00:00:59.068 --> 00:01:02.801
Of these, there were 20 deaf-blind with impairment

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.967
of both hearing and vision.

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:08.001
The use of more definitive diagnostic studies

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:11.567
and clinical observation showed that the 17 considered normal

00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:16.034
probably did not have the congenital rubella syndrome.

00:01:16.035 --> 00:01:19.968
Comprehensive evaluations were done on 80 children

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:22.568
at age two and a half years in order to project

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:24.368
their educational needs.

00:01:24.368 --> 00:01:28.168
Those with primarily an auditory or visual handicap

00:01:28.168 --> 00:01:31.501
were eligible for existing preschool programs.

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:33.834
The staff of the child development clinic

00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:37.135
initiated a diagnostic therapeutic nursery

00:01:37.135 --> 00:01:40.668
for the 25 multiply-handicapped children.

00:01:40.668 --> 00:01:45.368
Their arbitrary classification as mild, moderate, or severe

00:01:45.368 --> 00:01:48.160
was based on physical, developmental,

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.260
and behavioral deviations.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.335
The clinic offers interdisciplinary services

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:57.535
for the parents and child.

00:01:57.535 --> 00:02:00.401
Individual sessions with the social worker

00:02:00.401 --> 00:02:03.234
focus on current stresses produced by the presence

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.560
of a handicapped child in the family.

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:12.060
The persistence of rubella virus

00:02:12.068 --> 00:02:15.334
may cause progression of known physical abnormalities

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.220
and development of new ones.

00:02:21.301 --> 00:02:25.200
The pediatrician must therefore evaluate all systems

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.180
in his periodic examination.

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:36.535
During each regular eye examination,

00:02:36.535 --> 00:02:38.635
the ophthalmologist looks for evidence

00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:43.735
of congenital cataract, glaucoma, microphthalmus,

00:02:43.735 --> 00:02:46.701
and rubella retinopathy.

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.667
Similar in-depth studies are done

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:54.340
by the cardiologist, otologist, audiologist, and orthopedist.

00:02:57.201 --> 00:03:00.160
Maturation, cognition, and behavior,

00:03:00.168 --> 00:03:03.501
while related to the degree of structural abnormality,

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:07.301
cannot be defined by physical examination alone.

00:03:07.301 --> 00:03:10.201
Developmental and psychological profiles

00:03:10.201 --> 00:03:14.001
are used by the psychologist to determine the mental age

00:03:14.001 --> 00:03:18.867
and to identify specific areas of functional delay or deficit.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:21.734
The psychologists have selected different test instruments

00:03:21.735 --> 00:03:25.068
for three children of comparable chronological age

00:03:25.068 --> 00:03:28.134
based on prior knowledge of their medical disability

00:03:28.135 --> 00:03:31.268
and direct observation of their behavior.

00:03:31.268 --> 00:03:34.501
Excitability and inattention, rather than

00:03:34.501 --> 00:03:37.201
the severity of visual and hearing impairment,

00:03:37.201 --> 00:03:40.034
prevent this deaf-blind girl from performing

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.360
on the Gesell Developmental Test.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.068
The hearing-impaired, but otherwise normal child,

00:03:47.068 --> 00:03:51.001
is being tested with the non-verbal

00:03:51.001 --> 00:03:53.001
Leiter International Scale.

00:03:53.001 --> 00:03:56.967
Her intellectual potential, as well as affect and behavior,

00:03:56.967 --> 00:03:58.000
are appropriate.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.535
Performance on the Stanford-Binet

00:04:06.535 --> 00:04:09.968
indicates that this physically normal boy is also

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.068
of average intelligence.

00:04:12.068 --> 00:04:15.168
At five years of age however, immunological tests

00:04:15.168 --> 00:04:18.001
showed that his original diagnosis of rubella

00:04:18.001 --> 00:04:19.900
was probably incorrect.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.120
Motor skills, particularly creeping, crawling,

00:04:35.135 --> 00:04:38.601
and walking, did not develop in an orderly progression

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:41.867
for most of the congenital rubella patients.

00:04:41.868 --> 00:04:45.568
For some children, athetosis, spasticity,

00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:48.568
or hypotonia resulting from central nervous system

00:04:48.568 --> 00:04:52.201
involvement, was a significant handicap.

00:04:52.201 --> 00:04:55.567
This girl with spastic diplegia, in addition to

00:04:55.568 --> 00:04:58.434
correctable auditory and visual impairment,

00:04:58.435 --> 00:05:01.168
has had individual physical therapy

00:05:01.168 --> 00:05:04.868
and has been fitted with long leg braces and crutches.

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:07.168
Overcoming architectural barriers,

00:05:07.168 --> 00:05:10.600
such as stairs at school, is a part of her gait training.

00:05:13.401 --> 00:05:16.900
The earlier motor handicap of the boy at the table

00:05:16.901 --> 00:05:21.434
is not evident since he no longer needs a short leg brace.

00:05:21.435 --> 00:05:23.435
Both of these children have progressed

00:05:23.435 --> 00:05:25.535
from the multi-handicapped program

00:05:25.535 --> 00:05:28.568
to a pre-school deaf class.

00:05:28.568 --> 00:05:31.934
Their functional vision and auditory amplification

00:05:31.935 --> 00:05:35.501
allow them to interact and to respond with pleasure

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.540
to the sight and sound of the barking toy dog.

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:50.560
An area of baldness is seen on the back of the heads

00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:53.201
of many rubella children.

00:05:53.201 --> 00:05:56.201
This resulted from the type of bizarre movement

00:05:56.201 --> 00:05:59.101
and self-stimulatory activity demonstrated

00:05:59.101 --> 00:06:03.220
by this 18-month-old deaf-blind motor-delayed child.

00:06:08.967 --> 00:06:12.160
The multi-handicapped girl in the parallel bars

00:06:12.168 --> 00:06:15.601
has only minimal muscle weakness and is at a readiness stage

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:17.567
for walking.

00:06:17.568 --> 00:06:20.968
Her distractibility, short attention span,

00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:24.801
and her poor vision, even with contact lenses,

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:28.434
are preventing her independent locomotion.

00:06:28.435 --> 00:06:30.435
She later progressed to walking alone

00:06:30.435 --> 00:06:34.835
with one hand on the wall, but would not cross an open doorway

00:06:34.835 --> 00:06:38.568
until provided with a yardstick with which to maintain contact

00:06:38.568 --> 00:06:40.100
with the floor or wall.

00:06:49.301 --> 00:06:52.735
Hypotonia and muscle weakness, although initially

00:06:52.735 --> 00:06:56.035
present in many of the children, usually improved

00:06:56.035 --> 00:06:58.635
by the age of two and a half years.

00:06:58.635 --> 00:07:01.301
This severely hypotonic boy is still unable

00:07:01.301 --> 00:07:05.201
to hold his head erect at age four years.

00:07:05.201 --> 00:07:07.901
The physical therapist is positioning him so as

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:10.234
to utilize his minimal muscle power

00:07:10.235 --> 00:07:13.001
to help develop motor function.

00:07:13.001 --> 00:07:15.834
The therapist attempts to stimulate head control

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:18.268
by holding his attention with a flashlight

00:07:18.268 --> 00:07:20.601
and a spinning musical top.

00:07:20.601 --> 00:07:22.734
Head control would have to be established

00:07:22.735 --> 00:07:25.401
in the normal neurodevelopmental sequence

00:07:25.401 --> 00:07:29.301
before he could coordinate movements of his arms, legs,

00:07:29.301 --> 00:07:30.534
and trunk.

00:07:30.535 --> 00:07:33.068
His inability to maintain a given posture

00:07:33.068 --> 00:07:36.760
is fatiguing and soon results in loss of motivation.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.968
Although this little girl has normal vision

00:07:45.968 --> 00:07:48.901
and aided hearing within the speech range,

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.167
coordinated upper extremity movement

00:07:51.168 --> 00:07:55.368
is extremely difficult for her due to severe spasticity

00:07:55.368 --> 00:07:57.401
and athetosis.

00:07:57.401 --> 00:08:00.901
The occupational therapist is demonstrating an activity

00:08:00.901 --> 00:08:04.667
which requires the child to use the fine muscles of her hand

00:08:04.668 --> 00:08:08.534
and forearms for grasping, and the gross muscles

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.620
of her shoulders and arms for reaching and placing

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.440
the block on the pedestal.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.960
This demands a great effort from the child,

00:08:16.968 --> 00:08:20.434
who is rewarded by an approving facial expression

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.500
and verbal praise from the therapist.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.335
Psychomotor retardation and maladaptive behavior

00:08:32.335 --> 00:08:36.635
prevent establishment of the oral mechanisms of sucking,

00:08:36.635 --> 00:08:38.735
chewing, and swallowing,

00:08:38.735 --> 00:08:42.401
the essential antecedents of eating and drinking.

00:08:42.401 --> 00:08:45.434
The occupational therapist is attempting to teach this

00:08:45.435 --> 00:08:49.135
three-and-a-half-year-old boy each step in the hand-to-mouth

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.680
progression of self-feeding.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.900
First, he must learn to grasp the spoon or cup, hold onto it,

00:08:56.901 --> 00:09:00.367
and then move it through space from the table to his mouth

00:09:00.368 --> 00:09:04.534
with increasing dexterity and minimal spilling.

00:09:04.535 --> 00:09:08.501
He rejects not only the utensils, but any interaction

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:10.734
with the therapist herself.

00:09:10.735 --> 00:09:13.168
Even in children who are attentive,

00:09:13.168 --> 00:09:16.834
upper extremity weakness, lack of motor control,

00:09:16.835 --> 00:09:21.301
and poor eye-hand coordination may delay the acquisition

00:09:21.301 --> 00:09:22.920
of self-help skills.

00:09:25.201 --> 00:09:27.300
The speech therapist must consider

00:09:27.301 --> 00:09:30.467
both mental and chronological age in programming

00:09:30.468 --> 00:09:32.668
for multiply-handicapped children.

00:09:32.668 --> 00:09:34.534
She teaches the mothers of children

00:09:34.535 --> 00:09:38.035
under three years of age how to provide language stimulation

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:39.901
through daily activities.

00:09:39.901 --> 00:09:43.834
A bell is used by this mother to make her 18-month-old girl

00:09:43.835 --> 00:09:46.301
aware of environmental sounds.

00:09:46.301 --> 00:09:49.401
Such awareness is a forerunner to comprehension

00:09:49.401 --> 00:09:50.820
of the spoken voice.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.268
This three-year-old boy prefers to sight

00:09:54.268 --> 00:09:56.434
through the hole in the plastic ring

00:09:56.435 --> 00:09:58.968
rather than use the ring appropriately.

00:09:58.968 --> 00:10:01.234
When his mother has his attention,

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.340
he can comprehend her directions.

00:10:05.901 --> 00:10:09.235
A standing board is used for positioning and total body

00:10:09.235 --> 00:10:13.735
support of the hypotonic boy shown previously on the mat.

00:10:13.735 --> 00:10:16.968
This allows him to participate in a small group conducted

00:10:16.968 --> 00:10:19.134
by the speech therapist.

00:10:19.135 --> 00:10:22.968
He responds appropriately by laughing at the barking dog

00:10:22.968 --> 00:10:27.501
and attempts to use his arms and hands to grasp the color rings.

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:30.167
On the other hand, the deaf-blind girl

00:10:30.168 --> 00:10:33.401
shows no more interest in auditory stimulation

00:10:33.401 --> 00:10:35.820
than she did in independent locomotion.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.235
The nursery groupings for peer interaction

00:10:40.235 --> 00:10:42.635
are based on the degree of impairment

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:46.360
and the cumulative effect of multiple handicaps on behavior.

00:10:47.168 --> 00:10:50.900
Those classified as severe are immature

00:10:50.901 --> 00:10:53.801
and have inappropriate emotional responses,

00:10:53.801 --> 00:10:56.767
lack of environmental awareness and self-stimulation,

00:10:56.768 --> 00:11:00.034
which prevent optimal group participation.

00:11:00.035 --> 00:11:02.368
Many of these deaf-blind children

00:11:02.368 --> 00:11:05.868
are absorbed in stereotyped activities frequently referred

00:11:05.868 --> 00:11:07.868
to as blindisms.

00:11:07.868 --> 00:11:09.968
Waving their hand in front of their eyes

00:11:09.968 --> 00:11:12.934
seems to produce a hypnotic flicker effect.

00:11:12.935 --> 00:11:16.801
Jabbing the thumb or forefinger into the globe of the eye

00:11:16.801 --> 00:11:20.967
appears to be pleasurable rather than painful for them.

00:11:20.968 --> 00:11:22.934
The therapeutic program is designed

00:11:22.935 --> 00:11:27.235
to develop the child's attention span, his awareness of others,

00:11:27.235 --> 00:11:30.001
and his own self concept.

00:11:30.001 --> 00:11:32.534
This goal-directed activity is most difficult

00:11:32.535 --> 00:11:36.168
to achieve with these autistic-like children

00:11:36.168 --> 00:11:38.634
who do not interact with the group.

00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:42.168
For this reason, one-to-one behavior modification

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:44.700
techniques are a more successful training method.

00:11:47.401 --> 00:11:49.635
Children in the moderate group function

00:11:49.635 --> 00:11:51.668
at about the one-year language level

00:11:51.668 --> 00:11:54.534
by Gesell developmental norms.

00:11:54.535 --> 00:11:57.468
Although they are able to understand and execute

00:11:57.468 --> 00:12:00.800
a verbal command, their attention span is short.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:05.168
Distraction by pleasurable visual stimulation

00:12:05.168 --> 00:12:09.601
often prevents completion of the task directed by the therapist.

00:12:09.601 --> 00:12:13.734
Sighting through the translucent cup into a direct light results

00:12:13.735 --> 00:12:16.235
in amplification of the child's existing

00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:19.035
nystagmus and represents another example

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:20.960
of a pleasurable blindism.

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:32.068
The children in the mild multi-handicapped group

00:12:32.068 --> 00:12:35.168
are able to comprehend directions and carry out

00:12:35.168 --> 00:12:38.801
a fine motor activity emphasizing color matching

00:12:38.801 --> 00:12:41.001
and number concepts.

00:12:41.001 --> 00:12:42.867
This represents a level of function

00:12:42.868 --> 00:12:45.634
which approaches their chronological age.

00:12:45.635 --> 00:12:49.368
They have a longer attention span and watch and interact

00:12:49.368 --> 00:12:50.700
with the other children.

00:12:51.568 --> 00:12:55.500
Most have a moderate to severe neurosensory hearing loss,

00:12:55.501 --> 00:12:57.834
but have adequate vision to lip-read

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:01.700
and make maximal use of visual clues given by the teacher.

00:13:05.301 --> 00:13:08.135
Imagery play is used by the nursery teacher

00:13:08.135 --> 00:13:11.868
to initiate group interaction with three children.

00:13:11.868 --> 00:13:14.501
They are over three and a half years of age

00:13:14.501 --> 00:13:16.601
but are still at the parallel play

00:13:16.601 --> 00:13:18.420
stage of social development.

00:13:19.067 --> 00:13:21.600
The little boy was placed in the nursery program

00:13:21.601 --> 00:13:23.934
for habilitation of multiple handicaps

00:13:23.935 --> 00:13:26.268
resulting from head injury.

00:13:26.268 --> 00:13:29.801
Although his physical appearance and combination of handicaps

00:13:29.801 --> 00:13:31.601
is somewhat different from the children

00:13:31.601 --> 00:13:34.734
with congenital rubella, the therapeutic approach

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.480
is essentially the same.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.168
Over the past three and a half years,

00:13:41.168 --> 00:13:43.168
the parents and professional staff

00:13:43.168 --> 00:13:46.168
have mutually benefited from the discussion

00:13:46.168 --> 00:13:49.034
and educational groups focused on the management

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:51.000
of these handicapped children.

00:13:51.668 --> 00:13:53.860
The nursery teacher is demonstrating

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.920
how to make an edible play dough from flour, water, and food coloring.

00:14:01.268 --> 00:14:03.920
The speech therapist then shows the parents

00:14:03.935 --> 00:14:07.435
how they may use the play dough for language stimulation

00:14:07.435 --> 00:14:12.135
by the use of simple phrases such as "take it," "roll it,"

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:17.900
and "pull it," which are clearly enunciated so that the child can read their lips.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.468
Continuous monitoring and discussion

00:14:24.468 --> 00:14:28.434
of each child's progress at interdisciplinary staffings

00:14:28.435 --> 00:14:32.101
provides the basis for projecting short and long-term

00:14:32.101 --> 00:14:36.420
functional and educational goals for optimal habilitation.

